{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c827","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a087bb-8c16-7c41-9df2-bf65e2d2e1e6"},"sequence":622,"recorded_at":1780531400001239,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a087bd-389b-7b01-9853-8d4ed47609b0"},"task_id":"01a087bd-389b-7b01-9853-8d4ed47609b0","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a087bd-389b-7b01-9853-8d4ed47609b0","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(11867 line(s) above)\n11868|### 14.7 Session selector 1.0.0 and CLI Result 5.0.0\n11869|\n11870|This section is the v0.6.0 selector contract. It applies to the umbrella\n11871|<code>ax SELECTOR</code> and every existing managed-session NAME operand in\n11872|Section 14, including status, attach, takeover, fork, stop, resume, sync,\n11873|diff, materialize, logs --session and session set-profile. Creation NAME, destination HOST,\n11874|provider-native identifiers, Directory instance IDs/query expressions, and the\n11875|internal <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code> are not selector operands. No new\n11876|product operation or ownership authority is introduced.\n11877|\n11878|#### 14.7.1 Literal grammar and source-index resolution\n11879|\n11880|A selector is one CLI argument. Ordinary shell quoting handles spaces; the CLI\n11881|MUST NOT join multiple arguments, expand aliases as shell text, decode percent\n11882|escapes, normalize Unicode, trim, or case-fold configured host aliases. Split\n11883|at the FIRST literal <code>@</code> only. The key is either a Section 2 session\n11884|NAME or <code>id:SESSION_UUID</code>, with a canonical lowercase UUIDv7; neither\n11885|key form contains <code>@</code>. A bare UUID retains Section 2.3 name-first\n11886|precedence. <code>id:</code> explicitly bypasses names and never denotes a\n11887|provider-native ID, directory instance ID, lease ID, or UUID prefix.\n11888|\n11889|| Form | Meaning |\n11890|| --- | --- |\n11891|| <code>NAME</code> / bare UUID | Exact Section 2.3 local-name, peer-name, UUID, not-found precedence; collision refusal unchanged |\n11892|| <code>id:SESSION_UUID</code> | Durable AX session UUID in the local and allowed-peer union, independent of names |\n11893|| <code>KEY@local</code> | Only the local derived source index |\n11894|| <code>KEY@peer:ALIAS</code> | Only the configured peer source whose entire remaining suffix is the literal alias |\n11895|| <code>KEY@id:HOST_UUID</code> | Only the source whose canonical host UUID is given; local UUID selects local; otherwise exactly one allowlisted configured peer |\n11896|\n11897|Examples: <code>ax status build@local</code>,\n11898|<code>ax status build@peer:workstation</code>,\n11899|<code>ax status \"build@peer:Work Laptop\"</code>,\n11900|<code>ax status \"build@peer:Ops@east:თბილისი\"</code>, and\n11901|<code>ax status id:0198f4c8-3e70-7a11-8a2b-1234567890ab</code>.\n11902|A percent sign in an alias is literal. <code>peer:</code> with an empty alias,\n11903|unknown source prefixes, malformed durable IDs, and an empty key are invalid\n11904|arguments. The alias obeys the existing configured name bound, 1–64 characters;\n11905|this grammar adds no alias encoding, normalization, or registration path.\n11906|\n11907|Resolution first validates configuration, including uniqueness of exact alias\n11908|and host-ID mappings. Duplicate source mappings are invalid_config, never\n11909|first-match selection. An explicit unknown alias/host is selector_source_not_found;\n11910|a known but disallowed peer is peer_not_allowlisted. A failed, partial, malformed,\n11911|or inaccessible config read is invalid_config. A remote source transport/I/O read failure is selector_source_read_failed\n11912|(exit 8); a local source/store I/O failure is the existing\n11913|local_precondition_failed (exit 3). A partial or malformed index/authority\n11914|response is integrity_failure (exit 9), not absence. A complete valid empty index\n11915|or a complete index without the selected live key returns not_found. A malformed\n11916|record or digest failure is integrity_failure. These outcomes MUST remain distinct.\n11917|An explicit source MUST NOT fall back to the local index, another peer, a cached\n11918|index from another source, or a union search, even when the same name/UUID exists\n11919|there. A peer source requires a complete read of that peer's advertised index\n11920|under the selected transport, with verified configured host identity; a local\n11921|cached observation does not prove that read succeeded.\n11922|\n11923|For a qualified NAME, select in that source only: exact live name first, then\n11924|exact UUID if the NAME is UUID-shaped. Deduplicate multiple copies of the same\n11925|session UUID only when their immutable record digest agrees. Distinct live\n11926|UUIDs with the same name or ASCII-fold-colliding names return name_ambiguous.\n11927|For bare input retain exactly Section 2.3 tiers: a local exact match wins over a\n11928|peer exact match; a peer exact match wins over UUID interpretation. Collision\n11929|checks apply within the reached tier. Unread lower tiers need not be consulted\n11930|after a unique higher-tier match. A tier that must be read but cannot be read\n11931|is a read failure, not permission to proceed to a lower tier. Tombstoned entries\n11932|are excluded only with authoritative tombstone evidence, not a failed record read.\n11933|\n11934|#### 14.7.2 Resolve once, bind facts, revalidate execution\n11935|\n11936|Resolve the key to a session UUID once per invocation/confirmed plan. Build a\n11937|machine-local immutable SelectionPlan under selector contract 1.0.0 with exactly\n11938|the required members in this table. No plan hash, name, source, or display field\n11939|is a capability. A persisted plan has a locally verified canonical digest over\n11940|all members; caller-supplied digest equality alone is not evidence of authority.\n11941|\n11942|| Member | Type and binding |\n11943|| --- | --- |\n11944|| <code>selector_version</code> | Exact 1.0.0 |\n11945|| <code>selector</code> | Original single argument, at most 134 characters (64-character NAME + @ + peer: + 64-character alias) |\n11946|| <code>session_id</code> | Selected UUIDv7, never resolved again by name |\n11947|| <code>session_record_id</code> | Validated immutable Session Record digest for that UUID |\n11948|| <code>source_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 of the selected source; for a bare union match, provenance host of the selected entry, deterministic bytewise host-ID tie break for identical copies |\n11949|| <code>source_alias</code> | Exact configured alias or null for local; a diagnostic label, never identity |\n11950|| <code>source_index_digest</code> | Digest of the complete validated index read used for selection |\n11951|| <code>configuration_digest</code> | Digest of effective validated configuration, including peer mapping, allowlist and selected transport |\n11952|| <code>lease_record_id</code> | Validated winning Lease Record digest |\n11953|| <code>lease_epoch</code>, <code>lease_id</code>, <code>owner_host_id</code> | Positive uint53, UUIDv4, UUIDv7, all from that same winning lease |\n11954|| <code>authority_heads</code> | Sorted unique digest array of the validated lease/event/tombstone heads used for the decision |\n11955|| <code>action</code> | Explicit existing command/action tag selected under Sections 2.3 and 14 |\n11956|| <code>destination_host_id</code> | Explicit destination UUIDv7 or null when action has no destination; independently validated |\n11957|| <code>expectation_digest</code> | Digest binding the action's existing checkpoint/workspace/cohort/provider/terminal/confirmation expectations; does not replace those contracts |\n11958|\n11959|A source is an index location, never destination or proof of current ownership.\n11960|A local source can describe a remote owner; a peer source can describe a local\n11961|owner. Summary owner/role and execution routing MUST use the validated winning\n11962|lease and local-host comparison, never the source host or Session Record creator.\n11963|\n11964|Immediately before any effect, and again at every existing fencing/commit/retry\n11965|boundary, reread current configuration and required source data, validate the\n11966|same immutable Session Record, current tombstones and complete authority union,\n11967|winning lease and action prerequisites. Compare every bound plan fact. A changed\n11968|mapping, alias, allowlist, transport, source index, record, authority head, lease,\n11969|action, destination or expectation invalidates the plan. Return\n11970|selector_plan_stale before effects; explicit authorization revocation returns\n11971|peer_not_allowlisted; failed reads retain their read-failure classification.\n11972|Do not re-resolve by name, substitute a same-named session, silently replan,\n11973|transfer a lease, or reuse confirmation for new facts. An unrelated index change\n11974|may conservatively require a fresh plan. Reads revalidate before projecting output;\n11975|mutations retain existing serialized fencing/effect boundaries so a successful\n11976|precheck cannot authorize an effect after authority changes. If an earlier phase\n11977|already committed, use that operation's existing status/recovery contract with\n11978|its original IDs; a stale selection is not rollback or a new-operation retry.\n11979|\n11980|##### Selector execution boundaries\n11981|\n11982|The following action/boundary matrix is normative. Umbrella choices use their\n11983|selected action, never an umbrella bypass. `logs` applies when --session is\n11984|present; `cancel` only projects a choice. A boundary is reached only when the\n11985|existing operation reaches that phase; these rows do not introduce new effects.\n11986|`projection` precedes output, `pre-effect` precedes the first effect, `fencing`\n11987|covers every Section 5.3 fencing check, `commit` precedes durable publication,\n11988|`retry` and `recovery` precede continuation using original operation IDs.\n11989|`remote-dispatch` precedes sending attach expectations, `remote-admission`\n11990|precedes owner input, and `transport-resume` covers reconnect or sleep/transport\n11991|interruption under Section 5.3. None may skip checks because a previous phase\n11992|succeeded. Post-commit recovery checks current authority without demanding that\n11993|an operation's own authorized committed transitions equal its pre-commit plan:\n11994|reconcile those transitions through the original operation's status/receipts,\n11995|then build and validate its continuation expectations before any further effect.\n11996|Unexpected transitions refuse; they never authorize a new operation.\n11997|\n11998|| Selector action | Required boundary classes |\n11999|| --- | --- |\n12000|| status | projection, retry |\n12001|| attach | pre-effect, fencing, retry, remote-dispatch, remote-admission, transport-resume |\n12002|| takeover | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12003|| fork | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12004|| stop | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12005|| resume | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12006|| sync | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12007|| diff | projection, retry |\n12008|| materialize | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery |\n12009|| session.set-profile | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery |\n12010|| logs | projection, retry, remote-dispatch, remote-admission, transport-resume |\n12011|| cancel | projection |\n12012|\n12013|##### CLI 5 remote attach route\n12014|\n12015|For CLI Result 5 only, this paragraph supersedes the NAME forwarding and\n12016|re-resolution in Sections 13.4–13.5. The initiating host revalidates its complete\n12017|SelectionPlan at remote-dispatch, then invokes the argv-equivalent of:\n12018|\n12019|~~~shell\n12020|ssh -t HOST ax attach id:SESSION_UUID --local --result-version 5.0.0 --expect-selection EXPECTATIONS_JSON\n12021|~~~\n12022|\n12023|HOST is the verified configured endpoint for the plan's winning owner, never\n12024|the source. EXPECTATIONS_JSON is one canonically encoded JSON argument with\n12025|exactly the members/types in the following table. Transport invocation MUST\n12026|preserve argument boundaries with platform-appropriate shell quoting; no alias,\n12027|name or JSON value may become executable shell text. No application input is\n12028|forwarded until remote-admission succeeds. The selected configured transport\n12029|and its authentication/host-verification requirements remain mandatory; this\n12030|route does not weaken any stronger host-channel requirement or introduce an\n12031|RPC method. If that transport cannot carry the owner CLI attach stream with\n12032|its required authentication, refuse capability_unavailable before dispatch;\n12033|never downgrade to another transport. The receiver must support exact CLI 5;\n12034|an unsupported version refuses incompatible_schema, never retries legacy NAME\n12035|or bare UUID. CLI 1–4 retain the historical route and closed bytes.\n12036|\n12037|| Attach expectation | Type |\n12038|| --- | --- |\n12039|| selector_version | Exact 1.0.0 |\n12040|| session_id | UUIDv7 |\n12041|| session_record_id | digest |\n12042|| lease_record_id | digest |\n12043|| lease_epoch | Positive uint53 |\n12044|| lease_id | UUIDv4 |\n12045|| owner_host_id | UUIDv7 |\n12046|| authority_heads | digest array |\n12047|| expectation_digest | digest |\n12048|\n12049|These are expected values, not caller authentication or a capability. The\n12050|receiver validates them against its independently read exact session, winning\n12051|lease, complete authority and attach prerequisites; it uses a local plan with\n12052|its own source/configuration facts. The initiator's configuration digest or\n12053|source alias is never substituted for the receiver's local configuration.\n12054|Unknown/missing/extra expectation members or a non-id operand with this flag\n12055|are invalid_arguments; well-formed changed expectations are selector_plan_stale.\n12056|The flag is valid only for attach --local with CLI 5. A receiver that is not the\n12057|expected owner refuses selector_plan_stale rather than redirecting or taking\n12058|over. Both ends revalidate their own plans at their applicable boundaries.\n12059|Each end's plan action MUST equal attach and its destination MUST be null:\n12060|attach --local takes no destination argument, so a status plan, a plan for\n12061|another destination, or two agreeing copies of a wrong plan never authorize\n12062|attach input, even when that plan equals current. Two agreeing plan copies\n12063|prove nothing about the actual invocation.\n12064|The initiator plan MUST retain the literal single-argument selector and the\n12065|alias derived from the actually selected source (null for local): another\n12066|spelling that resolves to the same session UUID, or two agreeing copies of a\n12067|wrong selector/alias, never authorizes attach input. Receiver selector, alias\n12068|and source facts stay endpoint-local and are never cross-compared.\n12069|Receiver retry and transport-resume repeat exact-identity authority validation;\n12070|they never call NAME resolution. Receiver-local revocation/read/integrity\n12071|failures retain Section 14.7.1 classes and CLI 5/Error 1.4 binding. Caller\n12072|expectations cannot authorize input after revocation or a winning lease change.\n12073|\n12074|##### CLI 5 remote log filtering\n12075|\n12076|For CLI Result 5, the Section 14.1 remote logs NAME example and Result-1\n12077|validation are superseded by the following argv-equivalent. Resolve --session\n12078|once at the initiator and revalidate its plan before remote-dispatch. The\n12079|initiator refuses selector_plan_stale unless its plan session_id,\n12080|session_record_id and source_host_id equal the resolved selection;\n12081|plan/current agreement alone never proves selection binding:\n12082|\n12083|~~~shell\n12084|ssh HOST ax logs --session id:SESSION_UUID --limit N --json --result-version 5.0.0\n12085|~~~\n12086|\n12087|HOST is the explicit --peer log emitter, which may differ from both selection\n12088|source and winning owner. The receiver interprets id:SESSION_UUID as an exact\n12089|session-ID log filter, never a display name. It revalidates its local read plan\n12090|before projecting records; no ownership is granted and no record is created.\n12091|The initiating side validates exactly CLI Result 5 and the original command,\n12092|selected session filter, emitting_host_id and every returned event's host_id.\n12093|Session-scoped results contain only events for that exact session UUID. The\n12094|same Section 14.1 error classes, argument serialization, local-only remote\n12095|invocation and no-fan-out rules apply. Unsupported CLI5 refuses\n12096|incompatible_schema without retrying NAME, bare UUID or a lower result version.\n12097|A continuation cursor remains opaque to the initiator and is passed unchanged\n12098|to the same explicit --peer with --result-version 5.0.0. Its original durable\n12099|session filter is retained by the emitter; resume never re-resolves a name.\n12100|A cursor/emitter mismatch is invalid_arguments; forged emitter/event identity\n12101|is host_identity_mismatch/integrity_failure as in Section 14.1. Original plan\n12102|expectations must be revalidated at retry/transport-resume/projection boundaries.\n12103|Each end's plan action MUST equal logs. Each end's plan destination MUST equal\n12104|the actual explicit --peer host independently: the initiator's destination never\n12105|authorizes projection from another peer, and the receiver's destination is\n12106|checked against the same actual peer, never against the initiator's plan value.\n12107|A plan for another destination, or two ends agreeing on a wrong destination,\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c829","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a087bb-8c16-7c41-9df2-bf65e2d2e1e6"},"sequence":623,"recorded_at":1780531400001241,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a087bd-389b-7b01-9853-8d4ed47609b0"},"task_id":"01a087bd-389b-7b01-9853-8d4ed47609b0","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a087bd-389b-7b01-9853-8d4ed47609b0"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c82b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a087bb-8c16-7c41-9df2-bf65e2d2e1e6"},"sequence":624,"recorded_at":1780531400001243,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"a5a4bafa-7c8c-485e-ade1-b2a300d515d9"},"call_id":"call_01a087bd359c74219f944d126c4fd99e","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.6.0.md`.\n(11867 line(s) above)\n11868|### 14.7 Session selector 1.0.0 and CLI Result 5.0.0\n11869|\n11870|This section is the v0.6.0 selector contract. It applies to the umbrella\n11871|<code>ax SELECTOR</code> and every existing managed-session NAME operand in\n11872|Section 14, including status, attach, takeover, fork, stop, resume, sync,\n11873|diff, materialize, logs --session and session set-profile. Creation NAME, destination HOST,\n11874|provider-native identifiers, Directory instance IDs/query expressions, and the\n11875|internal <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code> are not selector operands. No new\n11876|product operation or ownership authority is introduced.\n11877|\n11878|#### 14.7.1 Literal grammar and source-index resolution\n11879|\n11880|A selector is one CLI argument. Ordinary shell quoting handles spaces; the CLI\n11881|MUST NOT join multiple arguments, expand aliases as shell text, decode percent\n11882|escapes, normalize Unicode, trim, or case-fold configured host aliases. Split\n11883|at the FIRST literal <code>@</code> only. The key is either a Section 2 session\n11884|NAME or <code>id:SESSION_UUID</code>, with a canonical lowercase UUIDv7; neither\n11885|key form contains <code>@</code>. A bare UUID retains Section 2.3 name-first\n11886|precedence. <code>id:</code> explicitly bypasses names and never denotes a\n11887|provider-native ID, directory instance ID, lease ID, or UUID prefix.\n11888|\n11889|| Form | Meaning |\n11890|| --- | --- |\n11891|| <code>NAME</code> / bare UUID | Exact Section 2.3 local-name, peer-name, UUID, not-found precedence; collision refusal unchanged |\n11892|| <code>id:SESSION_UUID</code> | Durable AX session UUID in the local and allowed-peer union, independent of names |\n11893|| <code>KEY@local</code> | Only the local derived source index |\n11894|| <code>KEY@peer:ALIAS</code> | Only the configured peer source whose entire remaining suffix is the literal alias |\n11895|| <code>KEY@id:HOST_UUID</code> | Only the source whose canonical host UUID is given; local UUID selects local; otherwise exactly one allowlisted configured peer |\n11896|\n11897|Examples: <code>ax status build@local</code>,\n11898|<code>ax status build@peer:workstation</code>,\n11899|<code>ax status \"build@peer:Work Laptop\"</code>,\n11900|<code>ax status \"build@peer:Ops@east:თბილისი\"</code>, and\n11901|<code>ax status id:0198f4c8-3e70-7a11-8a2b-1234567890ab</code>.\n11902|A percent sign in an alias is literal. <code>peer:</code> with an empty alias,\n11903|unknown source prefixes, malformed durable IDs, and an empty key are invalid\n11904|arguments. The alias obeys the existing configured name bound, 1–64 characters;\n11905|this grammar adds no alias encoding, normalization, or registration path.\n11906|\n11907|Resolution first validates configuration, including uniqueness of exact alias\n11908|and host-ID mappings. Duplicate source mappings are invalid_config, never\n11909|first-match selection. An explicit unknown alias/host is selector_source_not_found;\n11910|a known but disallowed peer is peer_not_allowlisted. A failed, partial, malformed,\n11911|or inaccessible config read is invalid_config. A remote source transport/I/O read failure is selector_source_read_failed\n11912|(exit 8); a local source/store I/O failure is the existing\n11913|local_precondition_failed (exit 3). A partial or malformed index/authority\n11914|response is integrity_failure (exit 9), not absence. A complete valid empty index\n11915|or a complete index without the selected live key returns not_found. A malformed\n11916|record or digest failure is integrity_failure. These outcomes MUST remain distinct.\n11917|An explicit source MUST NOT fall back to the local index, another peer, a cached\n11918|index from another source, or a union search, even when the same name/UUID exists\n11919|there. A peer source requires a complete read of that peer's advertised index\n11920|under the selected transport, with verified configured host identity; a local\n11921|cached observation does not prove that read succeeded.\n11922|\n11923|For a qualified NAME, select in that source only: exact live name first, then\n11924|exact UUID if the NAME is UUID-shaped. Deduplicate multiple copies of the same\n11925|session UUID only when their immutable record digest agrees. Distinct live\n11926|UUIDs with the same name or ASCII-fold-colliding names return name_ambiguous.\n11927|For bare input retain exactly Section 2.3 tiers: a local exact match wins over a\n11928|peer exact match; a peer exact match wins over UUID interpretation. Collision\n11929|checks apply within the reached tier. Unread lower tiers need not be consulted\n11930|after a unique higher-tier match. A tier that must be read but cannot be read\n11931|is a read failure, not permission to proceed to a lower tier. Tombstoned entries\n11932|are excluded only with authoritative tombstone evidence, not a failed record read.\n11933|\n11934|#### 14.7.2 Resolve once, bind facts, revalidate execution\n11935|\n11936|Resolve the key to a session UUID once per invocation/confirmed plan. Build a\n11937|machine-local immutable SelectionPlan under selector contract 1.0.0 with exactly\n11938|the required members in this table. No plan hash, name, source, or display field\n11939|is a capability. A persisted plan has a locally verified canonical digest over\n11940|all members; caller-supplied digest equality alone is not evidence of authority.\n11941|\n11942|| Member | Type and binding |\n11943|| --- | --- |\n11944|| <code>selector_version</code> | Exact 1.0.0 |\n11945|| <code>selector</code> | Original single argument, at most 134 characters (64-character NAME + @ + peer: + 64-character alias) |\n11946|| <code>session_id</code> | Selected UUIDv7, never resolved again by name |\n11947|| <code>session_record_id</code> | Validated immutable Session Record digest for that UUID |\n11948|| <code>source_host_id</code> | UUIDv7 of the selected source; for a bare union match, provenance host of the selected entry, deterministic bytewise host-ID tie break for identical copies |\n11949|| <code>source_alias</code> | Exact configured alias or null for local; a diagnostic label, never identity |\n11950|| <code>source_index_digest</code> | Digest of the complete validated index read used for selection |\n11951|| <code>configuration_digest</code> | Digest of effective validated configuration, including peer mapping, allowlist and selected transport |\n11952|| <code>lease_record_id</code> | Validated winning Lease Record digest |\n11953|| <code>lease_epoch</code>, <code>lease_id</code>, <code>owner_host_id</code> | Positive uint53, UUIDv4, UUIDv7, all from that same winning lease |\n11954|| <code>authority_heads</code> | Sorted unique digest array of the validated lease/event/tombstone heads used for the decision |\n11955|| <code>action</code> | Explicit existing command/action tag selected under Sections 2.3 and 14 |\n11956|| <code>destination_host_id</code> | Explicit destination UUIDv7 or null when action has no destination; independently validated |\n11957|| <code>expectation_digest</code> | Digest binding the action's existing checkpoint/workspace/cohort/provider/terminal/confirmation expectations; does not replace those contracts |\n11958|\n11959|A source is an index location, never destination or proof of current ownership.\n11960|A local source can describe a remote owner; a peer source can describe a local\n11961|owner. Summary owner/role and execution routing MUST use the validated winning\n11962|lease and local-host comparison, never the source host or Session Record creator.\n11963|\n11964|Immediately before any effect, and again at every existing fencing/commit/retry\n11965|boundary, reread current configuration and required source data, validate the\n11966|same immutable Session Record, current tombstones and complete authority union,\n11967|winning lease and action prerequisites. Compare every bound plan fact. A changed\n11968|mapping, alias, allowlist, transport, source index, record, authority head, lease,\n11969|action, destination or expectation invalidates the plan. Return\n11970|selector_plan_stale before effects; explicit authorization revocation returns\n11971|peer_not_allowlisted; failed reads retain their read-failure classification.\n11972|Do not re-resolve by name, substitute a same-named session, silently replan,\n11973|transfer a lease, or reuse confirmation for new facts. An unrelated index change\n11974|may conservatively require a fresh plan. Reads revalidate before projecting output;\n11975|mutations retain existing serialized fencing/effect boundaries so a successful\n11976|precheck cannot authorize an effect after authority changes. If an earlier phase\n11977|already committed, use that operation's existing status/recovery contract with\n11978|its original IDs; a stale selection is not rollback or a new-operation retry.\n11979|\n11980|##### Selector execution boundaries\n11981|\n11982|The following action/boundary matrix is normative. Umbrella choices use their\n11983|selected action, never an umbrella bypass. `logs` applies when --session is\n11984|present; `cancel` only projects a choice. A boundary is reached only when the\n11985|existing operation reaches that phase; these rows do not introduce new effects.\n11986|`projection` precedes output, `pre-effect` precedes the first effect, `fencing`\n11987|covers every Section 5.3 fencing check, `commit` precedes durable publication,\n11988|`retry` and `recovery` precede continuation using original operation IDs.\n11989|`remote-dispatch` precedes sending attach expectations, `remote-admission`\n11990|precedes owner input, and `transport-resume` covers reconnect or sleep/transport\n11991|interruption under Section 5.3. None may skip checks because a previous phase\n11992|succeeded. Post-commit recovery checks current authority without demanding that\n11993|an operation's own authorized committed transitions equal its pre-commit plan:\n11994|reconcile those transitions through the original operation's status/receipts,\n11995|then build and validate its continuation expectations before any further effect.\n11996|Unexpected transitions refuse; they never authorize a new operation.\n11997|\n11998|| Selector action | Required boundary classes |\n11999|| --- | --- |\n12000|| status | projection, retry |\n12001|| attach | pre-effect, fencing, retry, remote-dispatch, remote-admission, transport-resume |\n12002|| takeover | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12003|| fork | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12004|| stop | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12005|| resume | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12006|| sync | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery, transport-resume |\n12007|| diff | projection, retry |\n12008|| materialize | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery |\n12009|| session.set-profile | pre-effect, fencing, commit, retry, recovery |\n12010|| logs | projection, retry, remote-dispatch, remote-admission, transport-resume |\n12011|| cancel | projection |\n12012|\n12013|##### CLI 5 remote attach route\n12014|\n12015|For CLI Result 5 only, this paragraph supersedes the NAME forwarding and\n12016|re-resolution in Sections 13.4–13.5. The initiating host revalidates its complete\n12017|SelectionPlan at remote-dispatch, then invokes the argv-equivalent of:\n12018|\n12019|~~~shell\n12020|ssh -t HOST ax attach id:SESSION_UUID --local --result-version 5.0.0 --expect-selection EXPECTATIONS_JSON\n12021|~~~\n12022|\n12023|HOST is the verified configured endpoint for the plan's winning owner, never\n12024|the source. EXPECTATIONS_JSON is one canonically encoded JSON argument with\n12025|exactly the members/types in the following table. Transport invocation MUST\n12026|preserve argument boundaries with platform-appropriate shell quoting; no alias,\n12027|name or JSON value may become executable shell text. No application input is\n12028|forwarded until remote-admission succeeds. The selected configured transport\n12029|and its authentication/host-verification requirements remain mandatory; this\n12030|route does not weaken any stronger host-channel requirement or introduce an\n12031|RPC method. If that transport cannot carry the owner CLI attach stream with\n12032|its required authentication, refuse capability_unavailable before dispatch;\n12033|never downgrade to another transport. The receiver must support exact CLI 5;\n12034|an unsupported version refuses incompatible_schema, never retries legacy NAME\n12035|or bare UUID. CLI 1–4 retain the historical route and closed bytes.\n12036|\n12037|| Attach expectation | Type |\n12038|| --- | --- |\n12039|| selector_version | Exact 1.0.0 |\n12040|| session_id | UUIDv7 |\n12041|| session_record_id | digest |\n12042|| lease_record_id | digest |\n12043|| lease_epoch | Positive uint53 |\n12044|| lease_id | UUIDv4 |\n12045|| owner_host_id | UUIDv7 |\n12046|| authority_heads | digest array |\n12047|| expectation_digest | digest |\n12048|\n12049|These are expected values, not caller authentication or a capability. The\n12050|receiver validates them against its independently read exact session, winning\n12051|lease, complete authority and attach prerequisites; it uses a local plan with\n12052|its own source/configuration facts. The initiator's configuration digest or\n12053|source alias is never substituted for the receiver's local configuration.\n12054|Unknown/missing/extra expectation members or a non-id operand with this flag\n12055|are invalid_arguments; well-formed changed expectations are selector_plan_stale.\n12056|The flag is valid only for attach --local with CLI 5. A receiver that is not the\n12057|expected owner refuses selector_plan_stale rather than redirecting or taking\n12058|over. Both ends revalidate their own plans at their applicable boundaries.\n12059|Each end's plan action MUST equal attach and its destination MUST be null:\n12060|attach --local takes no destination argument, so a status plan, a plan for\n12061|another destination, or two agreeing copies of a wrong plan never authorize\n12062|attach input, even when that plan equals current. Two agreeing plan copies\n12063|prove nothing about the actual invocation.\n12064|The initiator plan MUST retain the literal single-argument selector and the\n12065|alias derived from the actually selected source (null for local): another\n12066|spelling that resolves to the same session UUID, or two agreeing copies of a\n12067|wrong selector/alias, never authorizes attach input. Receiver selector, alias\n12068|and source facts stay endpoint-local and are never cross-compared.\n12069|Receiver retry and transport-resume repeat exact-identity authority validation;\n12070|they never call NAME resolution. Receiver-local revocation/read/integrity\n12071|failures retain Section 14.7.1 classes and CLI 5/Error 1.4 binding. Caller\n12072|expectations cannot authorize input after revocation or a winning lease change.\n12073|\n12074|##### CLI 5 remote log filtering\n12075|\n12076|For CLI Result 5, the Section 14.1 remote logs NAME example and Result-1\n12077|validation are superseded by the following argv-equivalent. Resolve --session\n12078|once at the initiator and revalidate its plan before remote-dispatch. The\n12079|initiator refuses selector_plan_stale unless its plan session_id,\n12080|session_record_id and source_host_id equal the resolved selection;\n12081|plan/current agreement alone never proves selection binding:\n12082|\n12083|~~~shell\n12084|ssh HOST ax logs --session id:SESSION_UUID --limit N --json --result-version 5.0.0\n12085|~~~\n12086|\n12087|HOST is the explicit --peer log emitter, which may differ from both selection\n12088|source and winning owner. The receiver interprets id:SESSION_UUID as an exact\n12089|session-ID log filter, never a display name. It revalidates its local read plan\n12090|before projecting records; no ownership is granted and no record is created.\n12091|The initiating side validates exactly CLI Result 5 and the original command,\n12092|selected session filter, emitting_host_id and every returned event's host_id.\n12093|Session-scoped results contain only events for that exact session UUID. The\n12094|same Section 14.1 error classes, argument serialization, local-only remote\n12095|invocation and no-fan-out rules apply. Unsupported CLI5 refuses\n12096|incompatible_schema without retrying NAME, bare UUID or a lower result version.\n12097|A continuation cursor remains opaque to the initiator and is passed unchanged\n12098|to the same explicit --peer with --result-version 5.0.0. Its original durable\n12099|session filter is retained by the emitter; resume never re-resolves a name.\n12100|A cursor/emitter mismatch is invalid_arguments; forged emitter/event identity\n12101|is host_identity_mismatch/integrity_failure as in Section 14.1. Original plan\n12102|expectations must be revalidated at retry/transport-resume/projection boundaries.\n12103|Each end's plan action MUST equal logs. Each end's plan destination MUST equal\n12104|the actual explicit --peer host independently: the initiator's destination never\n12105|authorizes projection from another peer, and the receiver's destination is\n12106|checked against the same actual peer, never against the initiator's plan value.\n12107|A plan for another destination, or two ends agreeing on a wrong destination,\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
